---
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../components/SiteLayout.astro";

const query = `runnel find users -e local -d accounts \\
  --filter '{"active":true}' \\
  --projection '{"_id":0,"name":1}' --limit 2`;
const result = JSON.stringify(
  {
    ok: true,
    data: {
      env: "local",
      db: "accounts",
      collection: "users",
      format: "ejson",
      documents: [{ name: "Ada" }, { name: "Lin" }],
      truncated: true,
      truncationReason: "documents",
      limits: { documents: 2, bytes: 524288 },
    },
  },
  null,
  2
);
const install = `git clone https://github.com/abijith-suresh/runnel.git
cd runnel
npm ci
mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/share"
runnel_install="$HOME/.local/share/runnel-local"
npm run install:local -- "$runnel_install"
export PATH="$runnel_install/node_modules/.bin:$PATH"
runnel --version`;
---

<SiteLayout>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">
      <span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> A CLI for agents · MongoDB
    </p>
    <h1 id="intro">
      Database access,
      <br />
      <span>by name.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Register a connection once. Give your agent a database name and let it get to work.
    </p>
    <p class="hero-detail">
      Runnel maps environments and database aliases to MongoDB connections. Humans handle setup.
      Agents query, run JavaScript, and export results through the CLI.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="primary-link" href="#build">
        Build locally <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/abijith-suresh/runnel/blob/main/apps/cli/README.md"
      >
        Read the CLI docs <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="small-note">Early stage. Available from source, not yet published to npm.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={query} label="Query a named database" copy />
    <p class="terminal-note">
      <code>-e local</code> selects the environment. <code>-d accounts</code> selects its database
      alias.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="setup">
    <div class="chapter-heading">
      <span class="chapter-number" aria-hidden="true">
        01
      </span>
      <h2 id="setup">Name it once.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      A human runs <code>runnel setup</code> in an interactive terminal, enters a hidden MongoDB
      URI, then chooses databases and names their aliases. The user-wide catalog keeps the mappings;
      OS credential storage keeps the URI.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={`runnel setup`} label="Human setup, after local installation" copy />
    <p>
      Agents discover those names without supplying connection strings. Every database command needs
      an environment. You can omit the database alias only when that environment has exactly one.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      code={`runnel envs
runnel databases -e local
runnel list -e local -d accounts
runnel describe users -e local -d accounts`}
      label="Discover names, then inspect collections"
      copy
    />
    <aside class="margin-note">
      <span class="note-marker" aria-hidden="true">
        ↳
      </span>
      <p>
        A detached local worker owns the native database handles and connection pools. Handles stay
        in the worker and never cross IPC.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="workflow">
    <div class="chapter-heading">
      <span class="chapter-number" aria-hidden="true">
        02
      </span>
      <h2 id="workflow">Use the names. Keep the driver.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      Start with a query. Reach for a pipeline or an attached JavaScript module when the task needs
      more. MongoDB is available today; MCP and other providers are planned.
    </p>
    <dl class="command-list">
      <div>
        <dt>
          <code>find · count</code>
        </dt>
        <dd>Filter documents or count matches with JSON or EJSON inputs.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          <code>aggregate</code>
        </dt>
        <dd>Run a MongoDB pipeline unchanged under the database user's permissions.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          <code>run</code>
        </dt>
        <dd>Execute a local JavaScript module with native handles in the worker.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          <code>export</code>
        </dt>
        <dd>Save a bounded JSON or EJSON array to a new file.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <CodeBlock
      code={`runnel count users -e local -d accounts
runnel aggregate users -e local -d accounts \\
  --pipeline-file pipeline.json
runnel run lookup.mjs -e local -d accounts \\
  --args '{"filter":{"active":true}}'
runnel export users -e local -d accounts \\
  --output users.ejson`}
      label="Commands for the next step"
      copy
    />
    <CodeBlock
      code={`export default async function ({ db, args, signal }) {
  return await db.collection("users")
    .findOne(args.filter, { signal });
}`}
      label="lookup.mjs"
      copy
    />
    <aside class="margin-note">
      <span class="note-marker" aria-hidden="true">
        ↳
      </span>
      <p>
        Database permissions are authoritative. Scripts and aggregation pipelines can write. Runnel
        adds no CLI read/write approval gate, and result limits do not bound side effects.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="results">
    <div class="chapter-heading">
      <span class="chapter-number" aria-hidden="true">
        03
      </span>
      <h2 id="results">Read the result. Check the bounds.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      Query output defaults to canonical MongoDB EJSON, preserving BSON types and numeric widths.
      Find, aggregate, and export default to 100 documents, allow at most 1,000, and cap the
      document array at 512 KiB.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={result} label="Illustrative result for the query above" output />
    <p>
      Inspect <code>ok</code> and <code>data.truncated</code>. A successful result can still be
      incomplete; <code>data.truncationReason</code> explains whether it hit the document or byte
      limit. Exports use the same bounds and are not database backups.
    </p>
    <p class="small-note">
      The names and returned documents on this page are illustrative. Export destinations must be
      new files in existing directories.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="agent-guide">
    <div class="chapter-heading">
      <span class="chapter-number" aria-hidden="true">
        04
      </span>
      <h2 id="agent-guide">The agent instructions ship with it.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>runnel guide</code> reads the bundled offline instructions. Use{" "}
      <code>runnel skill path</code> to locate the installed <code>skills/runnel/SKILL.md</code> for
      your agent's skill mechanism. These commands need no catalog, credentials, database, or
      network.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      code={`runnel guide
runnel guide query
runnel guide scripts
runnel skill path`}
      label="Orient an agent"
      copy
    />
  </section>

  <section class="chapter build-section" aria-labelledby="build">
    <div class="chapter-heading">
      <span class="chapter-number" aria-hidden="true">
        05
      </span>
      <h2 id="build">Build locally.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      Use <strong>Node 24.19.0</strong>, <strong>npm 11.16.0</strong>, and Git on your path. Runnel
      has not been published. This POSIX shell example builds and installs the three public packages
      together from local tarballs.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={install} label="Build and install from source" copy />
    <p>
      The installation destination must be an absolute path to a <strong>new directory</strong>{" "}
      whose parent already exists. Existing files, directories, and links are refused. Choose
      another name if <code>runnel-local</code> exists. On Windows, the installation path and its
      resolved parent must exclude <code>&amp;</code>, <code>%</code>, <code>^</code>,{" "}
      <code>!</code>, and control characters. Keep compatible Node on your path, and add the
      installed <code>node_modules/.bin</code> to your shell profile for future terminals.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then run <code>runnel setup</code> yourself in an interactive terminal. Linux needs a session
      D-Bus connection and an unlocked Secret Service store. Native Windows uses Windows credential
      storage; its interactive setup and signal behavior still need platform validation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before switching Runnel builds, run <code>runnel daemon stop</code>, install into another new
      directory, and update your path. The{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/abijith-suresh/runnel/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#local-testing">
        local testing guide
      </a>{" "}
      covers direct compiled CLI use and Windows installation path rules.
    </p>
    <div class="closing-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/abijith-suresh/runnel">
        Browse the source <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a href="https://github.com/abijith-suresh/runnel/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">
        Contribute <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
